   Jingwei and Ali, Izhar and Yang, Zi and Jiang, Xiaoxuan and Chen, Yong
   and Xiong, Youcai and Wang, Yuxin and An, Lizhe and Chen, Shuyan and
   Xiao, Sa and Rai, Prabhat Kumar},
Title = {The negative effects of invasive plants on ecosystem multifunctionality
   weaken with increase in temperature},
Journal = {ECOLOGICAL INDICATORS},
Year = {2026},
Volume = {188},
Month = {JUL},
Abstract = {Invasive plants introduced outside their native ranges by human
   activities are posing significant threats to ecosystem functions.
   Nevertheless, the effects driven by plant invasions are scantily
   described in relation to the ability of ecosystems providing multiple
   functions simultaneously (i.e., ecosystem multifunctionality, EMF).
   Given that climate warming has been shown to influence the ecological
   consequences of invasive plants, how these impacts vary along
   temperature gradients also requires further in-depth investigation.
   Meanwhile, the impact of indigenous species provides a better reference
   for assessing the consequences of invasive species. In this study, we
   collected plant and soil samples from beneath either invasive Xanthium
   species (Xanthium spinosum L. and Xanthium italicum Moretti) or
   indigenous Xanthium species (Xanthium sibiricum Patr.) and their
   adjacent areas without Xanthium at 12 sites in northwest China. We found
   that invasive Xanthium was associated with lower EMF compared to the
   `without Xanthium' treatment, while indigenous Xanthium exhibited a
   non-significant upward trend. The negative association between invasive
   Xanthium and EMF became weaker with increasing temperature, while there
   was no significant interaction between indigenous Xanthium and warming.
   According to the results of piecewise structural equation modelling
   (pSEM), abiotic factors were more strongly associated with EMF responses
   in invaded ecosystems, whereas biotic factors showed stronger
   associations in indigenous ecosystems. Our study highlights that the
   negative association between invasive plants and EMF is less pronounced
   with increasing temperature. This may provide a theoretical basis for
   understanding the consequences of plant invasions and for managing
   invasive plants under different climatic conditions.},
